    "body": "## What\n\n`signAuthEntries` passes the `signerAddress` a wallet returns on to the default authorizer, as the key to check the signature against. A muxed `M…` address resolves to its base `G…` key. `null` or an empty string counts as omitted. Any other value that is not an account address is rejected with an error that names it (new internal helper `walletSigningKey` in `src/contract/signer.ts`). A custom `authorizeEntry` still gets raw bytes, as before. The contract auth guide gains a short section, \"When a wallet signs with a different key\".\n\nRefs #1681 (part 2 of that issue; part 1 is covered by #1743).\n\n## Why\n\n`SignAuthEntry` lets a wallet return `signerAddress` to say \"I signed this, but with a different key than the address being authorized\", which is the normal case for classic multisig. `signAuthEntries` dropped that field. The default authorizer then checked the signature against the account's own key and rejected a valid signature with \"signature doesn't match payload\". This broke x402 payments signed with a second registered signer.\n\nThe stricter handling is a small behavior change. Before, the SDK ignored any `signerAddress`. Now a wallet that reports a wrong `G…` key, or a value that is not an account address, fails locally. Both are wallet bugs under SEP-43, and failing with a clear error beats a signature that will not verify. The local check shows the key signed the payload. It does not show the key has enough weight on the account; the network still enforces that, and the guide says so.\n\nReview notes, left for follow-ups: `signAuth ?? Promise.resolve` near the callback is an existing bug on `main` (unbound `Promise.resolve`). A wrapped copy of the SDK's `authorizeEntry` counts as custom and still gets raw bytes.\n\nThis PR and #1743 edit adjacent hunks in `signAuthEntries` and its tests. Whichever merges second needs a small rebase.\n",
